# Phase 482: CLAUDE.md: rule 10 (composers done), rule 11 (composers on the shared timeline), rule 15 (26 waits for the
# combined review), the log row.
import sys, re
p = sys.argv[1]; s = open(p, encoding='utf-8').read()
def one(a, b):
    global s
    if s.count(a) != 1: raise SystemExit('not found once: ' + a[:60])
    s = s.replace(a, b)
one("Level 4 is done. Level 5: melody (Phase 481). Next (her 2026-", "Level 4 is done. Level 5: melody (Phase 481), composers (Phase 482). Next (her 2026-")
one("""   Workbook page 7 is any piece's melody. Next composers and styles on one timeline (26, 27), then more scales.""",
"""   Workbook page 7 is any piece's melody. Phase 482 built composers (26) on the timeline of music that chapter 27
   shares (MusTimeline, mtl482: one axis, 1600 to today; rows periods, keyboards, composers, sound, styles; each chapter
   draws its own rows strongly, the others lightly): page 1 the whole timeline, a lane per composer in birth order;
   pages 2 to 5 one period each (its stretch of the timeline and a who's who); page 6 who lived when (a line down from
   one year); the screen lesson ends with four composers to tap in order of birth; Workbook page 7 is my composers (any
   piece: composer, years, period, keyboard). Next styles (27) on the same timeline, then more scales.""")
one("23 (terms), 24 (form) and 25 (melody).", "23 (terms), 24 (form), 25 (melody) and 26 (composers).")
ROW = """| 482 | 2026-10-17 | **Chapter 26, Composers through Time (시대와 작곡가), built in full on a timeline of music that chapter 27 will share (her 09:42 order: composers, styles, genres and periods together).** It replaces the 2026 one (Phases 304-305's lesson, drill, Workbook and Teacher copy; their data CMP, CMP_PERIODS, CMP_HEAR and the six older tools stay). **The timeline** (`MusTimeline`, mtl482.jsx): one x axis 1600 to today; rows: periods (CMP_PERIODS: Baroque 1600-1750, Classical 1750-1820, Romantic 1820-1900, Modern 1900-1950, Contemporary 1950-), keyboards (harpsichord to 1770, fortepiano to 1830, piano; the first piano 1700; chapter 18's facts), composers (lives; a lane each in birth order, or packed), sound (one phrase a period) and styles (blues from 1890, ragtime 1895-1920, jazz from 1915, rock and pop from 1950; checked against the Library of Congress and Wikipedia); each chapter draws its own rows strongly; coral is the one thing looked at (a picked period or composer, the line down from one year); names that do not fit a band go under its row; `en` draws it in English for the Workbook; drawn on screen at its real width (useCpxW). **23 composers** (`CPX_PAGES`): Vivaldi, Bach, Handel, Scarlatti · Haydn, Clementi, Mozart, Beethoven · Schubert, Chopin, Robert Schumann, Liszt, Clara Schumann, Brahms, Tchaikovsky · Debussy, Joplin, Bartók, Prokofiev, Kabalevsky, Sculthorpe, Williams, Hisaishi, each with a short place and one piece (`CPX_FROM`, `CPX_PIECE`). **Lesson Book** (7 pages, dictionary entries): p1 the timeline ("A composer's period is when they wrote their music"; the sound and styles rows are chapter 27); p2-p5 one period each (its stretch of the timeline, a who's who; Baroque: counterpoint, harpsichord; Classical: fortepiano, Alberti bass; Romantic: rubato, virtuoso; Modern and Contemporary: Impressionism); p6 who lived when (contemporaries / 동시대인; a line down from 1785 crosses Haydn, Clementi, Mozart, Beethoven; at the piano Haydn's "Surprise" tune and "Ode to Joy"); p7 five checks (the periods in order, the keyboards, a composer and piece a period, who was alive in a year, my piece). **Screen**: the map pad (a period lights its composers and plays one; its composers as chips), a pad per period (tap a composer: their music and one fact), who was alive (a slider and five years), in order (four composers by birth). **Drill** (9): 1 period of a year, 2 period of a composer (not Beethoven, Debussy, Kabalevsky), 3 born first, 4 alive at the same time (10 years together or 5 apart), 5 who wrote it ("the music for" film), 6 listening: Baroque, Classical or Romantic (real openings only), 7 listening: who wrote it, 8 the words, 9 extra: three in order of birth; each answer shows the lives on the timeline; each level remembers its last questions (sheets do not repeat). **Workbook** p2-p5 draw the lives on a ruler, who wrote it (join), the words; p6 a line down from a year, who came first; p7 my composers (a table and a timeline to draw on); p8 check-up A-E (E: the keyboard of Bach, Mozart, Chopin); **Teacher copy** 2 pages. Toolbox: 7 new tools (cpx-map, cpx-baroque, cpx-classical, cpx-romantic, cpx-modern, cpx-alive, cpx-order; two new icons); chapter name Composers through Time / 시대와 작곡가; `REC_SINCE.composers` (this ship). printables.html: the chapter 26 card's words (the card image waits for her combined review). Reviewed by four strict reviewers (theory 17, Korean 31, design 15, code 17 findings; 3 blockers, all fixed) and a verifier (12 partly fixed and 14 new; all fixed but Schubert's clip, the Marche militaire, which the reveal names). For her: the period boundaries (a convention), 동시대인, the keyboards row's 1770 and 1830, Williams and Hisaishi as Contemporary, the sound row's words (chapter 27 will settle them). |
"""
m = re.search(r'^\| 481 \| [^\n]*\n', s, re.M)
if not m: raise SystemExit('row 481')
s = s[:m.end()] + ROW + s[m.end():]
open(p, 'w', encoding='utf-8').write(s); print('CLAUDE.md: rules 10, 11, 15; log row 482')
